  "reason": "R1 already applies high-rate MD tension at several temperatures to a closely related FCC Cantor-type alloy and identifies partial-dislocation-mediated deformation and local HCP-related structures. R2 supplies another atomistic uniaxial-loading study at 10^10 s^-1, making a strain-rate sweep from 10^8 to 10^10 s^-1 a plausible extension. R20 directly establishes the temperature-dependent intrinsic-stacking-fault and FCC/HCP energetics of the closely related CoCrFeMnNi system, while several other records connect temperature, dislocation activity, and strength. Thus an expert could plausibly propose orientation-, temperature-, and rate-dependent MD tension and analyze partial dislocations and stacking faults. The vacancy-cluster nucleation site, exact orientation ranking, rate-insensitive yield strength, and absence of twinning or bulk transformation are not specifically anticipated, but they are refinements or outcomes of an otherwise reconstructible study."
